00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.450 align:start position:0%
 
hello welcome back now let's return to  our top story around 100 000 civil  servants uh to go on strike next month  in a significant escalation of their  dispute with the government over jobs  pay and conditions the public and  Commercial Services union says its  members in some 124 government  departments and other bodies will walk  out for 24 hours on the 1st of February  it'll be the biggest industrial action  within the Civil Service in years let's  bring in the General Secretary of the  PCS Union Mark sawatke who joins us now  live uh good evening to you Mark Walker  thank you so much for being with us this  evening so why have you announced this  action now to begin on the 1st of  February  uh well good evening Kamali  um well as we've been I think saying now  for many weeks  um we represent the government's own  Workforce and the government are  treating its own Workforce worse than  anyone else in the economy we've been  given a two percent pay rise we've been  told tens of thousands of jobs are going  to be slashed and our conditions are  going to be cut  and we represent people who deliver  public services from cradle to grave  whether it's Universal Credit collecting  tax overseeing the minimum wage keeping  our justice system running our borders  open issuing passports we were clapped  by the government doing the pandemic and  yet now after 10 years of pay cuts we've  been told it's two percent and even  though the government know that 40 000  of our civil servants use food banks  forty five thousand of them claim  benefits because they pay us so low and  today it was revealed that tens of  thousands of our members working civil  servants will get a pay raise because  the national minimum wage is increasing  which tells us how lowly paid we are  despite everything we've tried so far  and despite action in The Border force  over Christmas with driving tests being  canceled rural payments as agency being  affected the dvla being affected they've  still told us not a penny extra so we're  escalating the strike Now by bringing  out a hundred thousand people across 124  government departments for 24 hours  there will be further sustained action  announced in the coming days and what  we're saying to the government is put  some money on the table and we can avert  these strikes but if you don't this  action will take place and it will  escalate further now Mark you've had the  mandate to be able to pull out your  members uh since November and that's my  point to why now you've got talks coming  with the government this coming week  with Jeremy Quinn  um you're demanding a 10 raise and uh  also an increase to living wage for all  your members you've had this mandate for  a while I'm wondering why now why you  called for the first of February what  has it got to do with the TUC National  rights to strike day  yes it has but but first of all Kamali  we believe in saying to the government  look first of all we'd rather talk than  go on strike when they told us no we  said to them okay well we'll start our  strike action it's going to be  significant but if you continue to  ignore us the action will escalate and  sadly despite thousands of members  already being on strike and causing real  disruption we know the security of this  country was compromised over Christmas  because Army Personnel are not trained  to do passport checks properly they  still are maintaining a position not a  penny extra and our executive met today  and we're conscious that there's  meetings taking place across the rest of  the public sector there's talks of the  government may be finding money to put  on the table for others and this is us  saying you can't forget your own  dedicated Frontline civil servants and  we've announced a strike but of course  the strike is three weeks away and if  the meeting with Jeremy Quinn tomorrow  gives us an indication that money is  available these strikes can be averted  but if he maintains a position of no  extra money for for people who are poor  the inward poor working for the  government at the moment claiming  benefits using food banks then he needs  to know that all of our members will  come out on strike there will be immense  disruption everything from driving tests  to passports to the ports and airports  benefit offices job centers right across  the UK will be closed and if he wants to  avoid that disruption he needs to find  money and start negotiating now you'll  know that Mr Queen has talked about uh  wanting to hear a fair reasonable demand  from you guys I mean I'm sure you've  done the figures how much a 10 uh pay  rise and that a raising of all work is a  15 pounder work living wage would would  cost the government  yeah the government claims that if every  civil servant was given what we're  asking for it would cost 2.4 billion  pounds but that's the way of the  government trying to avoid a sensible  argument we believe that if you get less  than the rate of inflation you're  actually poorer for going to work at the  end of the year than you were at the  start of the year and I cannot be right  but what we've said to the government is  tell us money is available and we will  get into talks and if there's  compromises in those talks let's see  what actually happens but at the moment  it is not a penny more and that's why  we're taking strike action if the  government come up with some money we of  course are happy to talk to them about  how that's distributed and how much it  is but we also want them to agree not to  cut tens of thousands of jobs and to  stop robbing civil servants of their  contractual rights to their pensions I  think that we can have a constructive  dialogue but it's only if the government  agrees to find money and Kamali I want  to make the point that even if it's true  that it was to cost 2.4 billion pounds  that is a tiny fraction of what the  conservative government was prepared to  borrow to give tax cuts to the richest  in this country so the government knows  it can afford it it knows if they give  us a pay raise our members will spend it  in the local economy boosting  manufacturing and the hospitality sector  at a time of recession unlike the rich  who avoid paying taxes and put their  money in the Cayman Islands so it makes  sense for the economy it's just and it  makes sense to ensure that civil  servants are not working but are in  poverty now Mark as you said this does  have something to do with the fact that  the TSC are having a national right to  strike day on the the 1st of February  and that is as a result of those minimum  service level demands that have been  brought in by the government  um that legislation is going to take a  while to come through but you said that  previous strikes for Border staff  security was compromised so so why is it  wrong to have a minimum service standard  in the in the Border Force then  well it's wrong because it fundamentally  breaches people human rights we have a  right to strike we're not slaves people  go to work and they have the right to  withdraw their labor and what we're  saying to the government is the answer  is not to conscript people to work it is  to negotiate and what we all know is  that if the government was negotiating  these strikes would not be taking place  but the answer to a dispute is not  increasingly Draconian legislation that  removes people's fundamental rights it's  to recognize that the government needs  to put money and Mark let me just let me  just pick up on that because you know  the government will say that the right  to strike is not going away they just  want to ensure that there's a minimum  service level so what's your answer to  that idea you said that security was  compromised by not having the right  border for staff on the borders during  the previous strike  yes but that was because the government  insisted on bringing untrained people in  to do the job of professionals and what  we need to remember here is people  wouldn't go on strike without a  grievance but of course the point of  strike action is that it has an effect  it's no good saying the government  saying you have a right to strike but we  don't want anyone to notice because then  people are losing money and they're not  putting pressure on the employers to do  the right thing you know the odds are  always stacked against working people we  already have the toughest laws of any  European country it's incredibly  difficult to have a legal strike now  what the government is trying to do is  to say well you can have your strikes  but they shouldn't have any effect and  that really undermines people's ability  to use the threat of withdrawing their  labor to force employers to be more  reasonable and whether that's the  government or it's a private sector  employer we supposedly live in a  democracy and in a democracy people have  the right to withdraw their labor which  is why we oppose the legislation but I  would remind you Kamali that we saw  leaked emails that actually told us that  government seriously considered Banning  unions and making strikes illegal across  large ways of the public sector they  chose not to do that in the end but they  only chose not to do it because they  thought they would lose legally in court  that tells us a lot about the government  it does not support the right to strike  it actually considered Banning people  from joining trade unions and that is  not the actions of people who seriously  want to compromise the last question  there mark this is going to happen a  huge uh strike action taking place the  PCS on the 1st of February on the same  day as the TUC call for National rights  to strike day as you'll know the general  Striker is very difficult to call but is  this the Stars that's what we're going  to see is this how you guys are going to  escalate this dispute by having this  kind of coordinated action big  coordinate election on certain days  I think that we're seeing an  unprecedented amount of strike action  we've seen action today by paramedics  there may be nurses we've already seen  our rail our post and our civil service  I think as long as the government  retains its position of refusing to put  money on the table we will see more and  more cooperation and coordination  between unions I don't believe we would  be the only Union on strike on February  the 1st and I think if the government  maintains its current position more and  more unions will take action together in  the weeks ahead okay Mark sawuck ahead  of the PCS students appreciate your time  listening thank you for being with us  thank you

